From 4e27fef307a54d77e32898071ae2b22357675fae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude Opus 5.5" <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:17:51 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] pci: do not blacklist MSI in a VM that has no host bridge

pci_msi_blacklisted() treats every chipset without PCI Express or PCI-X
capabilities as unable to do MSI.  In a virtual machine it whitelists
old host bridges known to work, looking at the device at 0:0:0, and
otherwise blacklists MSI and MSI-X for the whole system.

A Hyper-V generation 2 VM, and the WSL2 utility VM in particular, has
no host bridge at all: its only PCI buses are the vPCI buses provided by
the hypervisor, which support MSI and MSI-X only.  Unless one of the
devices happens to have a PCI Express capability (as SR-IOV VFs do),
every device was denied MSI-X; the conventional PCI virtio console that
WSL2 attaches over vPCI then failed to set up its interrupts ("exhausted
all interrupt allocation attempts").

In a VM, do not blacklist MSI when there is no device at 0:0:0: there
is no old chipset to distrust.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/dev/pci/pci.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sys/dev/pci/pci.c b/sys/dev/pci/pci.c
index ba28f2468..e05fc8e00 100644
--- a/sys/dev/pci/pci.c
+++ b/sys/dev/pci/pci.c
@@ -2602,6 +2602,15 @@ pci_msi_blacklisted(void)
 			if (dev != NULL)
 				return (!pci_has_quirk(pci_get_devid(dev),
 					PCI_QUIRK_ENABLE_MSI_VM));
+
+			/*
+			 * No host bridge at all: the only PCI buses are
+			 * provided by the hypervisor, as in a Hyper-V
+			 * generation 2 VM or the WSL2 utility VM, whose vPCI
+			 * buses support MSI and MSI-X only.  There is no old
+			 * chipset to distrust.
+			 */
+			return (0);
 		}
 		return (1);
 	}
